  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Rare Bo Diddley opener, monster Lovelight jam, stellar blues",
    "blurb": "Features a unique Bo Diddley opening set followed by high‑energy second set with highlights like “How Sweet It Is (To Be Loved By You)”, a 22‑minute “Turn On Your Lovelight” jam, and Pigpen’s blues on “Smokestack Lightning”. The soundboard recordings capture the band in tight form, making this one of the most sought‑after early‑’72 shows.",
    "review": "The March 25, 1972 Academy of Music gig kicks off with a blistering Bo Diddley blues medley—“Hey Bo Diddley”, “Mannish Boy (I'm A Man)”, and a wild jam—that instantly sets an adventurous tone. The band then rolls into two full Dead sets where the energy stays high; Jerry flashes that trademark grin on the Bo Diddley numbers, launches fiery solos on “How Sweet It Is (To Be Loved By You)” and “Sitting On Top Of The World”, while Phil drives the groove with urgent bass lines that anchor the sprawling “Turn On Your Lovelight” twenty‑two minute instrumental. Pigpen’s harp roars through “Smokestack Lightning” and “Bertha”, and his rhythm guitar adds extra bite on the Lovelight finale. Keith sprinkles subtle keyboard textures, even a pedal‑steel tease on “Looks Like Rain”, giving the set an extra color palette. The Miller soundboard capture is crystal clear, letting every nuance—from Bob’s bluesy harp to Bill’s solid drumming—shine through, making this show a must‑listen for anyone chasing those rare early‑’70s moments.",
